STEP 1 — BUILD A CANDIDATE LEDGER

Review every identified phrase.

For each phrase, decide whether it contributes to:

- Class
- Attribute
- Association
- Inheritance
- Aggregation
- Association Class
- Attribute value / multiplicity only
- No separate model element

Do not force every identified phrase to become its own class or relationship.


STEP 2 — CLASS VS ATTRIBUTE

For every noun or noun phrase, apply the independent-existence test from the Classification Rules.

CLASS:
- has independent existence in the application domain;
- represents an identifiable domain object, actor, record, or concept.

ATTRIBUTE:
- cannot meaningfully exist independently;
- describes another object;
- stores scalar or state information.

Do not create two separate classes for terms that the description explicitly presents as two names or roles for the same underlying concept.

If two terms refer to the same concept, choose one canonical class name based on the wording used most consistently as the domain object in the description.


STEP 3 — ATTRIBUTE OWNERSHIP

Every attribute must have a supported owner.

Determine the owner from the sentence where the phrase occurs.

For an X-of-Y expression:
- normally classify X as an attribute of Y when Y is a class.

For other descriptive phrases:
- attach the attribute only to the class explicitly described by the source text.

Do not guess an attribute owner.


STEP 4 — VALUES AND QUANTITIES

Adjectives, enumerations, states, and quantities usually do NOT become classes.

Use them as:
- attribute values,
- state values,
- constraints,
- or multiplicities

when supported.

Do not output them as separate classes unless the description clearly gives them independent existence.


STEP 5 — ASSOCIATIONS

A meaningful transitive verb normally indicates an association.

Format:

as(<label>, <classA>, <classB>)

Determine the two endpoints from the actual sentence in the Domain Description.

Rules:
- both endpoints must already qualify as classes;
- use the actual participating domain concepts;
- do not substitute a related class merely because it seems reasonable;
- do not create an association from a verb if the source does not establish two supported class participants;
- use one concise relationship label based on the source wording.


STEP 6 — POSSESSION

For each possession expression, decide whether it represents:

1. an Attribute,
2. an Association, or
3. an Aggregation.

Use the source meaning, not the word “has” alone.

If the possessed thing has independent existence, consider an Association or Aggregation.

If it is merely descriptive scalar information, classify it as an Attribute.


STEP 7 — AGGREGATION

Use aggregation only for an explicit whole-part relationship.

Format:

ag(<whole>, <part>)

The part must be a domain object that can reasonably exist as an object, not merely an attribute value.


STEP 8 — INHERITANCE

Use inheritance only when the description provides an explicit IS-A, type-of, specialization, or equivalent relationship.

Format:

isa(<child>, <parent>)

Apply both tests from the Classification Rules:

1. IS-A test:
   every instance of the child must also be an instance of the parent.

2. Conformance test:
   relationships applicable to the parent should also make sense for the child.

Do not infer inheritance merely because two concepts are related.


STEP 9 — ASSOCIATION CLASS CHECK

Check EVERY association for a possible association class.

Create an association class only when BOTH conditions are true:

1. the underlying association is many-to-many; and
2. the Domain Description requires information about the relationship itself to be stored.

If both are true, create:

<AssociationClassName>(<ClassA>, <ClassB>)

Relationship-specific information should belong to the association class rather than being incorrectly attached to either endpoint.

Do not omit an association class merely because the same relationship can also be written as an ordinary association.


STEP 10 — CONSISTENCY AUDIT

Before answering, verify:

- no two classes represent the same domain concept;
- every class has independent existence;
- every attribute has the correct owner;
- every association uses the correct participants from the source sentence;
- every inheritance passes the IS-A and conformance tests;
- every aggregation is truly whole-part;
- every association has been checked for the two-condition association-class rule;
- no unsupported model elements were added;
- no important identified phrase was ignored without a modeling reason.


STRICT OUTPUT FORMAT

CLASSES:
